%[text] # Part 1 - From an Equation to Hardware: Thinking in Model-Based Design
%[text] **Audience:** RTL / Verilog engineers moving to Simulink. **Goal:** take a filter equation, build it in Simulink with the GUI, verify it, make it bit-accurate, and generate Verilog from it.
%[text] In an RTL flow the spec, the C/MATLAB reference model, the RTL and the testbench are four separate artifacts that must be kept in sync by hand. In Model-Based Design (MBD) the Simulink model **is** the executable spec, the golden reference and the source of the RTL. You verify the algorithm first (in floating point), then decide the bit widths, then let HDL Coder write the Verilog.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%%
%[text] ## 0. The mindset shift: Verilog concepts in Simulink
%[text:table]
%[text] | You write this in Verilog | You draw this in Simulink |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | `module fir (...)` | A **Subsystem** block - this becomes the DUT (Design Under Test) |
%[text] | `always @(posedge clk) q <= d;` | **Unit Delay** block ($ z^{-1} $) - one register |
%[text] | `assign y = a + b;` / combinational logic | Blocks connected by signal lines (Add, Gain, Switch, Logical Operator ...) |
%[text] | `reg signed [15:0] x;` and a Q1.15 comment | Data type `fixdt(1,16,15)` - the binary point is part of the type |
%[text] | `parameter` / `localparam` | MATLAB variable (here in the **Model Workspace**) used in block dialogs |
%[text] | Clock | Sample time. One Simulink step = one clock (enable) |
%[text] | Reset | Implicit. HDL Coder adds it (synchronous or asynchronous is a setting) |
%[text] | Testbench, `$display`, waveform viewer | Top-level model with sources, Scope and the Simulation Data Inspector |
%[text:table]
%[text] Rule of thumb: **draw the dataflow, not the hardware**. You do not draw clock or reset wires; HDL Coder creates them.
%%
%[text] ## 1. The equation
%[text] A 4-tap low-pass FIR filter:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ y\[n\] = b\_0\\,x\[n\] + b\_1\\,x\[n-1\] + b\_2\\,x\[n-2\] + b\_3\\,x\[n-3\], \\qquad b = \\frac{1}{8}\[1\\;\\,3\\;\\,3\\;\\,1\] $
%[text] The coefficients are binomial, so the filter has a triple zero at Nyquist: a tone at 0\.40 cycles/sample is attenuated by about 30 dB, a tone at 0\.01 cycles/sample passes with gain 1. Each $ x\[n-k\] $ is a register, each $ b\_k $ is a constant multiplier and the sum is an adder tree. That is exactly what we will draw.
%%
%[text] ## 2. Create a blank model
%[text] 1. On the MATLAB **Home** tab click **Simulink**. The Simulink Start Page opens.
%[text] 2. Click **Blank Model**, then **Save** it as `fir_mbd.slx` \
%[text] ![Simulink Start Page](text:image:a86201)
%%
%[text] ## 3. Make one simulation step equal one clock
%[text] RTL is cycle based, so we use a discrete, fixed-step solver with a step of 1.
%[text] 1. **Modeling** tab > **Model Settings** (or Ctrl+E).
%[text] 2. **Solver** pane: Type = `Fixed-step`, Solver = `discrete (no continuous states)`.
%[text] 3. Expand **Solver details** and set Fixed-step size = `1`. Set **Stop time** = `300`.
%[text] 4. Click **OK** \
%[text] ![Configuration Parameters - Solver](text:image:432c02)
%%
%[text] ## 4. Store the coefficients as data, not as wires
%[text] Like a Verilog `parameter`, keep design constants in one place.
%[text] 1. **Modeling** tab > **Model Explorer**.
%[text] 2. In the left tree select `fir_mbd` > **Model Workspace**.
%[text] 3. **Add** > **MATLAB Variable** three times and set: `b = [1 3 3 1]/8`, `f_sig = 0.01`, `f_noise = 0.40` \
%[text] The variables are saved inside `fir_mbd.slx` and every block dialog can use them.
%%
%[text] ## 5. Build the test bench first
%[text] Open the **Library Browser** (**Simulation** tab > **Library Browser**) and drag in the blocks below. Tip: you can also click on the canvas and just type the block name.
%[text] ![Library Browser - Simulink/Discrete](text:image:56fa03)
%[text:table]
%[text] | Block (library) | Name | Settings |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Sine Wave (Sources) | `Signal` | Sine type `Time based`, Amplitude `0.5`, Frequency `2*pi*f_sig`, Sample time `1` |
%[text] | Sine Wave (Sources) | `Noise` | Amplitude `0.4`, Frequency `2*pi*f_noise`, Sample time `1` |
%[text] | Add (Math Operations) | `Add` | default `++` |
%[text] | Data Type Conversion (Signal Attributes) | `To Q1.15` | Output data type `double` for now |
%[text] | Subsystem (Ports & Subsystems) | `FIR_Filter` | this is the DUT, built in the next step |
%[text] | Discrete FIR Filter (Discrete) | `Reference (Discrete FIR)` | Coefficients `b` - an independent golden model |
%[text] | Subtract (Math Operations), Scope (Sinks) | `Error`, `Scope` | Scope: 3 input ports, layout 3x1 |
%[text:table]
%[text] Connect them as shown. Right-click a signal > **Log Selected Signals** to log `x`, `y_dut`, `y_ref` and `err` to the Simulation Data Inspector.
%[text] ![Test bench with an empty DUT](text:image:790d04)
%%
%[text] ## 6. Draw the equation inside the DUT
%[text] Double-click `FIR_Filter` and draw the direct-form structure:
%[text] - 3 x **Unit Delay** (Discrete library) in a chain: $ x\[n-1\] $, $ x\[n-2\] $, $ x\[n-3\] $ - these are your `x_d1/x_d2/x_d3` registers.
%[text] - 4 x **Gain** (Math Operations) with Gain = `b(1)` ... `b(4)`. Right-click > **Rotate & Flip** to point them down.
%[text] - 3 x **Add** to form the adder chain, and rename the ports `x[n]` and `y[n]` \
%[text] ![The equation drawn with blocks](text:image:d10e05)
%[text] The Gain dialog shows the resolved value of the parameter next to the expression, so you always see what `b(2)` evaluates to:
%[text] ![Gain block uses the workspace variable](text:image:cbcb06)
%%
%[text] ## 7. Simulate in floating point - verify the math first
%[text] Click **Run**, then double-click the Scope. The noisy input `x` comes out clean as `y_dut`, and `err` (our filter minus the Discrete FIR reference) is exactly zero.
%[text] ![Scope - floating point](text:image:7fec07)
%[text] This is the step most RTL flows skip or do in a separate C model. Here it costs one click and it uses the same model you will generate code from.
%[text] Optional check from code (the finished model is in `models/fir_mbd.slx`):
demoRoot = fileparts(matlab.desktop.editor.getActiveFilename);
addpath(fullfile(demoRoot, "models"));
open_system("fir_mbd")
out = sim("fir_mbd");
maxError = max(abs(out.logsout.get("err").Values.Data))
%%
%[text] ## 8. Let the Fixed-Point Tool propose the bit widths
%[text] In Simulink a fixed-point type is written `fixdt(Signed, WordLength, FractionLength)`, so Q1\.15 is `fixdt(1,16,15)` and Q4\.18 is `fixdt(1,19,18)`. Once a model uses them, simulation is **bit-true**: it computes exactly what the hardware will compute, including truncation and overflow.
%[text] In RTL you pick every width by hand from experience. The **Fixed-Point Tool** does it from data instead: it simulates the floating-point design, records the min/max of every signal, and proposes types that cover those ranges. The starting point is `models/fir_mbd_float.slx`: the 16-bit input interface (`To Q1.15`) is already decided, as it would be for an ADC bus, and everything inside `FIR_Filter` is still `double`.
open_system("fir_mbd_float")
%[text] **(1) Open the tool and select the design.** **Apps** tab > **Fixed-Point Tool** > **New** > **Iterative Fixed-Point Conversion**. Under *System Under Design*, click `FIR_Filter` so that the tool converts only the DUT and leaves the test bench alone.
%[text] ![Fixed-Point Tool - select the system under design](text:image:837c08)
%[text] **(2) Collect ranges.** Open the **Collect Ranges** drop-down, choose **Double precision** (the tool temporarily overrides every type with `double`), then click **Collect Ranges**. The model simulates once.
%[text] ![Collect Ranges with double-precision override](text:image:058209)
%[text] The result table lists the simulation min/max of every block output. The histograms show how many bits each signal really uses.
%[text] ![Ranges collected](text:image:13e610)
%[text] **(3) Proposal settings.** Under **Settings** you choose the policy: propose fraction lengths for a default word length (here 16 bits), a safety margin on the observed range, and whether `double`/inherited types are converted.
%[text] ![Proposal settings](text:image:1e1211)
%[text] **(4) Propose Data Types.** The **Proposed DT** column fills in. Note the coefficients: they are always positive, so the tool proposes **unsigned** `fixdt(0,16,17)` for `b1`, which gives one more bit of precision than a signed 16-bit word. Click any row to see why: the range used, the precision, and potential overflows or underflows.
%[text] ![Proposed data types](text:image:5e9a12)
%[text] ![Result details for one block](text:image:09be13)
%[text] **(5) Apply Data Types.** The accepted proposals are written into the block dialogs. The *Specified DT* column changes from `double` to the fixed-point types.
%[text] ![Types applied to the model](text:image:a36214)
%[text] **(6) Verify.** Click **Simulate with Embedded Types**, then **Compare Results**. The Simulation Data Inspector overlays the floating-point run and the fixed-point run and plots the difference.
%[text] ![Embedded run - no overflows](text:image:f9a615)
%[text] ![Overflow vs underflow in fixed point](text:image:bb8416)
%[text] ![Floating point vs 16-bit fixed point in the Data Inspector](text:image:e8b917)
%[text] With a 16-bit datapath the worst-case error is about $ 7\\times10^{-5} $. That is around 2 LSB of the Q1\.15 output, and it includes quantizing the input itself. Whether that is acceptable is now a **numerical requirement** you can set as a tolerance in the tool, not a gut feeling. If you need a bit-exact result, raise the word length, or apply the exact Q4\.18 datapath by hand as in the next step.
%[text] Close the model without saving to keep `fir_mbd_float.slx` as a floating-point starting point.
%%
%[text] ## 9. Or set the bit widths by hand - the `reg signed [15:0]` step
%[text] Because this filter is tiny, we also know the exact answer: coefficients that are multiples of 1/8 on a Q1\.15 input give results that are exact in Q4\.18. This is what the hand-written Verilog in Part 2 uses. To set it in `fir_mbd`:
%[text] **(1)** `To Q1.15` block: Output data type = `fixdt(1,16,15)`, Integer rounding = `Nearest`, tick **Saturate on integer overflow**
%[text] ![Data Type Conversion to Q1.15](text:image:8f5118)
%[text] **(2)** Each Gain: **Parameter Attributes** tab > Parameter data type = `fixdt(1,16,15)`; **Signal Attributes** tab > Output data type = `fixdt(1,19,18)`
%[text] ![Gain coefficient data type](text:image:152319)
%[text] **(3)** Each Add: **Signal Attributes** tab > Output data type = `fixdt(1,19,18)`.
%[text] **(4)** **Debug** tab > **Information Overlays** > **Port Data Types** to see the types on every wire, then **Run** again: `err` is still zero, so 19 bits with 18 fraction bits are enough (the coefficients are multiples of 1/8, so nothing is lost)
%[text] ![Fixed-point datapath with port data types](text:image:ec8420)
%[text] ![Finished test bench](text:image:e97221)
%%
%[text] ## 10. Generate Verilog
%[text] **(1)** Optional micro-architecture choice: right-click each Gain > **HDL Code** > **HDL Block Properties** > `ConstMultiplierOptimization = csd`. The constant multipliers become shift-and-add, which is what you would hand-write.
%[text] **(2)** **Apps** tab > **HDL Coder**. On the **HDL Code** tab click **Settings** and select: Generate HDL for `fir_mbd/FIR_Filter`, Language `Verilog`, folder `hdlsrc`
%[text] ![HDL Code Generation settings](text:image:f31f22)
%[text] **(3)** Click **Generate HDL**. With **Report** > Traceability enabled you get a report where every line of Verilog links back to its block
%[text] ![HDL Code Generation Report](text:image:670723)
%[text] An excerpt of the generated `hdlsrc/fir_mbd/FIR_Filter.v` - registers, CSD shift-add and the adder chain, all 19-bit:
%[text] ```verilog
%[text] always @(posedge clk)
%[text]   begin : z_1_1_process
%[text]     if (reset == 1'b1) begin
%[text]       z_1_1_out1 <= 16'sb0000000000000000;
%[text]     end
%[text]     else begin
%[text]       if (enb) begin
%[text]         z_1_1_out1 <= x_n;
%[text]       end
%[text]     end
%[text]   end
%[text] // CSD Encoding (12288) : 011000000000000; Cost (Adders) = 1
%[text] assign b1_1 = {{3{z_1_1_out1[15]}}, {z_1_1_out1, 13'b0000000000000}};
%[text] assign b1_2 = {{4{z_1_1_out1[15]}}, {z_1_1_out1, 12'b000000000000}};
%[text] assign b1_mul_temp = b1_1 + b1_2;
%[text] assign b1_out1 = b1_mul_temp[30:12];
%[text] ```
%[text] On the Arty A7 build in Part 4 this filter uses **0 DSP slices** because of the CSD setting.
%%
%[text] ## 11. Control logic: finite state machines with Stateflow
%[text] Datapaths are drawn with blocks. Control logic - the `case (state)` blocks you write in every design - is drawn with **Stateflow**: you draw the state diagram you would normally sketch on a whiteboard, and that diagram is the executable, synthesizable FSM.
%[text] Example: a debounced level detector on the filter output. `det` goes high only after `y > TH` for 4 consecutive samples, and goes low only after `y < TH` for 4 consecutive samples. In RTL that is a state register, a counter, and a next-state `case`.
open_system("fir_fsm")
%[text] ![Filter + detector test bench](text:image:432024)
%[text] **(1) Add a chart.** Inside the DUT subsystem, Library Browser > **Stateflow** > **Chart** (or click the canvas and type *Chart*). Connect `y` in and `det` out.
%[text] ![DUT: datapath + control](text:image:2e5825)
%[text] **(2) Draw the states and transitions.** Double-click the chart. Drag states from the left palette, type the name and actions into each state, then drag from one state edge to another to create a transition and type its condition in square brackets.
%[text] ![The FSM in Stateflow](text:image:761226)
%[text] **(3) Define the data.** **Modeling** tab > **Symbols Pane** > **Resolve Undefined Symbols**, then set the types:
%[text:table]
%[text] | Symbol | Scope | Type | Verilog equivalent |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `y` | Input | Inherit (sfix19\_En18 from the filter) | `input signed [18:0] y` |
%[text] | `det` | Output | `boolean` | `output reg det` |
%[text] | `cnt` | Local | `uint8` | `reg [7:0] cnt` |
%[text] | `TH` | Constant | `fixdt(1,19,18)` = 0\.25 | `localparam` |
%[text] | `N` | Constant | `uint8` = 3 | `localparam` |
%[text:table]
%[text] Set **Chart properties** > *Action Language* = `MATLAB`. The numbers 1 and 2 on transitions leaving the same state are their **priority** (the `if / else if` order).
%[text] **(4) Simulate.** Run the model. During simulation the active state is highlighted in the chart (animation), and you can set breakpoints on states or transitions just like a debugger.
%[text] ![Filter output and detector output](text:image:19d927)
%[text] **(5) Generate Verilog.** Generate HDL for `fir_fsm/FIR_with_Detector` exactly as in step 10. HDL Coder turns the chart into the classic two-process FSM - state register plus combinational next-state `case` - that you would write by hand:
%[text] ```verilog
%[text] case ( is_Level_Detector)
%[text]   state_type_is_Level_Detector_IN_ARM_HI :
%[text]     begin
%[text]       if (y <= TH) begin
%[text]         is_Level_Detector_next = state_type_is_Level_Detector_IN_LOW;
%[text]         det_reg_next = 1'b0;
%[text]       end
%[text]       else if (cnt >= N) begin
%[text]         is_Level_Detector_next = state_type_is_Level_Detector_IN_HIGH;
%[text]         det_reg_next = 1'b1;
%[text]       end
%[text]       else begin
%[text]         ...   // cnt_next = cnt + 1 (saturating)
%[text]       end
%[text]     end
%[text]   state_type_is_Level_Detector_IN_HIGH :
%[text]     begin
%[text]       det_reg_next = 1'b1;
%[text]       if (y < TH) begin
%[text]         is_Level_Detector_next = state_type_is_Level_Detector_IN_ARM_LO;
%[text]         cnt_next = 8'd0;
%[text]       end
%[text]     end
%[text] ```
%[text] What you get compared with hand-coding: the diagram **is** the documentation; conflicting transitions and out-of-range data are flagged while you simulate; and coverage tools (Simulink Coverage) show which states and transitions the test bench actually exercised. Adding a state means drawing one box and two arrows, not reworking a `case` statement and its documentation.
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text:table]
%[text] | RTL flow | MBD flow you just did |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | Write a C/MATLAB reference, then hand-translate to RTL | One model: floating-point algorithm, then fixed-point, then generated RTL |
%[text] | Verify RTL by writing an HDL testbench | Verify in Simulink with the same test bench at every stage |
%[text] | Change a coefficient: edit RTL, re-verify | Change `b`, re-run, regenerate |
%[text] | Bit widths decided while coding | Bit widths decided by simulation and the Fixed-Point Tool |
%[text] | FSM written as `case` statements, documented separately | FSM drawn in Stateflow; the diagram is the code and the documentation |
%[text:table]
%[text] **Next:** Part 2 shows what to do with Verilog you already have.

